---
import LearningApps from '../components/LearningApps.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';import HeroArt from '../components/HeroArt.astro';import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';import {lessons} from '../lib/lessons.mjs';import {blockLessons} from '../lib/blocks/curriculum.mjs';
const more=[['/adventures/','Robot coding games'],['/studio/','Pixel art maker'],['/debug-detective/','Bug detective quiz'],['/playground/','Python playground'],['/blocks/playground/','Block coding playground'],['/projects/','Python projects'],['/glossary/','Coding glossary'],['/one-hour-coding/','One-hour coding plan']];
---
<Layout><section class="wrap hero"><div><h1><span class="pill hero-pill">✦ KidsCode · Free coding for kids</span>Learn to code.<br/>Make a little<br/><em>awesome.</em></h1><p>KidsCode is a free playground for curious minds. Guide a robot, snap code blocks together, make pixel art, and write real Python, right in your browser. Big ideas, tiny steps.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button dark" href="/adventures/">Let’s start playing <span>→</span></a><a class="button ghost" href="/explore/">Find my adventure</a></div><div class="hero-proof"><span>Free to explore</span><span>No sign-up</span><span>Ages 7 and up</span></div></div><HeroArt/></section><div class="marquee"><span>Dream it.</span><b>✦</b><span>Try it.</span><b>✦</b><span>Oops? Tweak it.</span><b>✦</b><span>You made it!</span><b>✦</b><span>Do it all again.</span></div>
<section class="wrap" style="padding-top:42px"><div class="cta-band" style="background:var(--mint)"><div><span class="eyebrow">NEW · A whole world of coding ideas</span><h2>Meet your programming<br/>building blocks.</h2><p>Variables, if/else, loops, lists, and more. Learn by snapping real programs together in 20 playful missions.</p></div><a class="button dark" href="/blocks/">Explore the block trail →</a></div></section><section class="wrap section"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">A little curiosity goes a long way</span><h2>Fun coding activities<br/>for kids, from the first click.</h2></div><p>Start with a robot. Make your own art. Type your first line of Python. There’s more than one way to become a maker.</p></div><div class="grid3"><a class="card card-link tint-green" href="/adventures/"><div class="card-icon">↗</div><h3>Give Bit a mission</h3><p>Tell a little robot where to go. Collect stars and discover the magic of instructions and loops.</p><div class="topic-list"><span>New to coding</span><span>8 missions</span></div><div class="card-footer">Code adventures <span>↗</span></div></a><a class="card card-link tint-peach" href="/studio/"><div class="card-icon" style="background:#ffbb93">▦</div><h3>Think in pixels</h3><p>A tiny square can be the start of something big. Build colorful pixel art, one coordinate at a time.</p><div class="topic-list"><span>Creative explorers</span><span>Make & download</span></div><div class="card-footer">Pixel studio <span>↗</span></div></a><a class="card card-link tint-yellow" href="/python/"><div class="card-icon" style="background:#ffcf58">&gt;_</div><h3>Meet real Python</h3><p>Make your code talk, think, and repeat. Learn by changing real programs and seeing what happens.</p><div class="topic-list"><span>Ready to type</span><span>10 lessons</span></div><div class="card-footer">Python trail <span>↗</span></div></a></div></section>
<section class="section-soft"><div class="wrap section demo-band"><div class="demo-copy"><span class="eyebrow">Your first “I did that!”</span><h2>A little code.<br/>A big hello.</h2><p>Computers need instructions. This one says: “show my message.” Choose a message and see what happens when you run it.</p><div class="steps"><div class="step"><div><h3>Pick an idea</h3><p>Every great thing starts with a little “what if?”</p></div></div><div class="step"><div><h3>Try. Tinker. Try again.</h3><p>Mistakes are clues. Follow them somewhere fun.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="demo-panel"><span class="pill">A tiny taste of Python</span><label for="hello-message">What should Bit say?</label><select id="hello-message"><option>I made this!</option><option>Hello, little universe!</option><option>Beep boop. Let's build!</option></select><pre><code id="hello-code">print("I made this!")</code></pre><button class="button dark" id="hello-run">▶ Run my message</button><div class="demo-result" id="hello-output" aria-live="polite">Your message lands here. ✨</div><p class="tiny">A preview of what <code>print()</code> does. Ready for real code? <a href="/python/hello-world/">Try lesson one →</a></p></div></div></section>
<section class="wrap section"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Made by you, obviously</span><h2>Don’t just play.<br/>Make the thing.</h2></div><a class="text-link" href="/projects/">See all projects ↗</a></div><div class="grid3"><a class="card card-link" href="/playground/?project=story"><span class="pill peach">WORDS + A LITTLE WEIRD</span><h3>A silly story machine</h3><p>An otter. The Moon. Too many pancakes. Your code gets to tell the story.</p><div class="card-footer">Python project <span>→</span></div></a><a class="card card-link" href="/debug-detective/"><span class="pill">PUT YOUR THINKING HAT ON</span><h3>Become a bug detective</h3><p>Spot the tiny mistakes. Predict what happens. Collect a few “aha!” moments.</p><div class="card-footer">8 playful puzzles <span>→</span></div></a><a class="card card-link" href="/playground/?project=pet"><span class="pill yellow">YOUR NEW LITTLE SIDEKICK</span><h3>Build a pocket robot</h3><p>Give it a name, some energy, and a personality. Then teach it a new trick.</p><div class="card-footer">Python project <span>→</span></div></a></div></section>
<section class="wrap"><div class="cta-band"><div><span class="eyebrow">No perfect code required</span><h2>You bring the imagination.<br/>We’ll bring the starting line.</h2></div><a class="button dark" href="/explore/">Find your next thing →</a></div></section>
<section class="wrap section" id="about-kidscode"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">What is KidsCode?</span><h2>Free coding for kids,<br/>one small step at a time.</h2></div><p>KidsCode is a free website where children learn programming by playing, building, and experimenting. Everything runs in a web browser. There is nothing to install and no account to create.</p></div><div class="grid3"><div class="card"><h3>Who it’s for</h3><p>Kids from about age 7 who wonder how computers follow instructions. Robot missions and pixel art need no typing. The Python trail suits confident readers and typists, often ages 10 and up. Grown-ups are welcome to tag along.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>What kids learn</h3><p>Sequences, loops, variables, if/else decisions, comparisons, lists, dictionaries, functions, and debugging. Many ideas show up twice: first as blocks you snap together, then as real Python you type.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>How it works</h3><p>Pick an activity and press Run. Progress, drafts, and pixel art are saved in the browser on your device. KidsCode works on computers, Chromebooks, and tablets; the block editor likes a bigger screen.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="wrap section" id="all-lessons"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Every lesson in one place</span><h2>Pick a trail.<br/>Start anywhere.</h2></div><p>Two step-by-step coding courses for kids, plus games and creative tools. Each lesson takes about 5–12 minutes.</p></div><div class="grid2 lesson-index"><div class="card tint-blue"><h3><a href="/blocks/">Block coding for kids</a></h3><p>{blockLessons.length} visual missions, from your first instruction to a complete algorithm.</p><ol>{blockLessons.map(l=><li><a href={'/blocks/'+l.slug+'/'}>{l.topic}</a></li>)}</ol></div><div class="card tint-yellow"><h3><a href="/python/">Python for kids</a></h3><p>{lessons.length} short lessons with real code that runs in your browser.</p><ol>{lessons.map(l=><li><a href={'/python/'+l.slug+'/'}>{l.topic}</a></li>)}</ol><h3>More ways to play</h3><ul>{more.map(([href,label])=><li><a href={href}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul></div></div></section>
<section class="wrap"><LearningApps/></section>
<section class="wrap section"><Faq items={[["Is KidsCode free?","Yes. Every activity, lesson, and playground on KidsCode is free to use. There are no paid levels, subscriptions, or locked lessons."],["Do I need to know anything about coding?","Nope! Start with Code Adventures. You can tap commands to guide Bit, with no typing needed. When you’re ready, the Python trail starts at the very beginning."],["What ages is KidsCode for?","The visual activities are designed with roughly ages 7–10 in mind. The Python trail suits confident readers and typists, often ages 10 and up. These are starting points, not rules: pick what feels interesting and ask a grown-up for a hand."],["Do kids need an account?","No. There is no sign-up or login. Completed activities and your playground draft can be saved in this browser on this device. Clearing browser data removes that progress, and it does not sync to other devices."],["Can I really run Python here?","Yes. The Python playground runs real Python in your browser. Nothing to install. The first run downloads the Python engine, so it needs an internet connection and may take a moment."],["Is KidsCode like Scratch?","It’s a similar idea: you snap blocks together instead of typing. KidsCode’s block lessons are built with Blockly and focus on programming foundations such as variables, decisions, loops, and lists. You can turn your blocks into Python when you’re ready. KidsCode is independent and is not part of Scratch."],["Which devices work best?","A computer, Chromebook, or tablet with an up-to-date web browser. Robot missions, pixel art, and the Python lessons also work on phones. The block editor has more room on a bigger screen."]]}/></section>
<script>const select=document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('#hello-message')!;const code=document.querySelector('#hello-code')!;select.addEventListener('change',()=>code.textContent='print('+JSON.stringify(select.value)+')');document.querySelector('#hello-run')?.addEventListener('click',()=>{document.querySelector('#hello-output')!.textContent=select.value;});</script>
</Layout>
